<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulick Fionn Burke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1509">1509</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-burgh">House of Burgh</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulick Fionn Burke was Mac William Uachtar, lord of Clanricarde in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a>, from 1485 until his death in 1509. In 1504 he led an alliance with the O'Briens and other Gaelic lords against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-fitzgerald-8th-earl-of-kildare">Gerald FitzGerald, 8th Earl of Kildare</a>, the Lord Deputy of Ireland, and was heavily defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-knockdoe">Battle of Knockdoe</a> near Galway, one of the largest battles fought in medieval Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulick-fionn-burke.jpg" alt="Ulick Fionn Burke" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self-created · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burke_(Clanricarde).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulick Fionn Burke was the son of Ulick Ruadh Burke, 5th Clanricarde, whom the Annals of the Four Masters call heir of the Earl of Ulster and a general patron of the learned of Ireland. Succeeding him in 1485 as 6th Clanricarde, he at once led an army into Machaire-Chonnacht and Hy-Many, burning and destroying corn and towns and demolishing the castle and prison of Tulsk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Burke was an especially aggressive warlord who sought authority over his fellow Bourkes in north Connacht, over the Ui Maine and the Síol Muireadaigh, and over the independent towns of Athenry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a>. Gerald FitzGerald, 8th Earl of Kildare, tried to restrain him by marrying him to his daughter, Lady Estacia FitzGerald, but could not stop him taking other wives, among them Slaine Ni Con Mara, who died in 1498. In 1500 he took the castle of Athleague and handed it to the sons of William O'Kelly, and with O'Conor he seized the castle of Tulsk.</p><p>In 1501 he seized Edmond, son of Rickard Burke, on his return from the pilgrimage of St. James in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and exacted a great ransom. By the early 1500s he was the pre-eminent lord in Connacht and an ally of Toirdhealbhach Donn Ó Brien, King of Thomond, which made him a threat both to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> and to Hugh Dubh O'Donnell, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">Tír Chonaill</a>. In 1503 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-ceallaigh">O'Kelly</a> and demolished three of his castles, Garbh-dhoire, Muine-an-mheadha and Gallach; O'Kelly, whose wife was openly Burke's lover, complained to the Lord Justice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>With Burke's capture of Athenry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> the quarrel became a national matter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a> gathered Ó Donnell of Tír Conaill, Ó Neill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tir-eoghain">Tír Eoghain</a>, Ó Connor Roe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macdermot">MacDermot</a> of Moylurg, Bourke of north Connacht and many lords of the Pale, while Burke was backed by Ó Brian and MacNamara of Thomond, Ó Carroll of Ely, Ó Kennedy of Ormond and several gallowglass units. After his defeat in August 1504 the annals record little of him until his death in 1509. His younger brother, Richard Óge Burke, succeeded him, and all but three of the later Clanricardes and Earls of Clanricarde were his descendants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulick Fionn Burke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their battles included Knockdoe in 1504, where <b>Ulick Fionn Burke</b> was defeated by the Earl of Kildare.</blockquote><a href="/state/clanricarde">Clanricarde</a></li></ul></section>
